From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 03:15:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] docs: document BaseConfig, the shared settings store

Explain the SharedPreferences-backed property pattern each setting follows and
how every app subclasses BaseConfig to add its own settings while inheriting the
shared ones. Comment-only.
---
 .../org/fossify/commons/helpers/BaseConfig.kt    | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/commons/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/commons/helpers/BaseConfig.kt b/commons/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/commons/helpers/BaseConfig.kt
index 66dfae5d4..165070c75 100644
--- a/commons/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/commons/helpers/BaseConfig.kt
+++ b/commons/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/commons/helpers/BaseConfig.kt
@@ -18,6 +18,22 @@ import java.util.LinkedList
 import java.util.Locale
 import kotlin.reflect.KProperty0
 
+/**
+ * The shared settings store every Fossify app builds on.
+ *
+ * `BaseConfig` wraps a single `SharedPreferences` instance and exposes each
+ * persisted setting as a Kotlin `var` whose getter reads the pref (with a
+ * default) and whose setter writes it back — so calling code treats settings as
+ * ordinary properties (`baseConfig.primaryColor = ...`) and never touches keys
+ * or `SharedPreferences.edit()` directly. The keys are the `String` constants in
+ * `Constants` (e.g. `PRIMARY_COLOR`); the defaults come from Commons resources.
+ *
+ * It is `open` on purpose: each app subclasses it (e.g. `class Config : BaseConfig`)
+ * to add its own settings in exactly this pattern, inheriting all the shared
+ * ones (theme colors, storage paths and SAF/OTG tree URIs, font choice, run
+ * count, etc.). Obtain the right subclass instance through the `Context.baseConfig`
+ * / `Context.config` extension rather than constructing it directly.
+ */
 open class BaseConfig(val context: Context) {
     protected val prefs = context.getSharedPrefs()
 
